WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.600
Scenario 5 walkthrough: Claude Code for Continuous Integration.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:15.988
A CI reviewer that cries wolf gets ignored, so this scenario is about running Claude Code headless and writing review prompts that flag real problems with few false positives.

3
00:00:17.388 --> 00:00:21.795
Scenario five puts Claude Code into a continuous integration pipeline.

4
00:00:21.795 --> 00:00:26.831
On each pull request it reviews the code, may generate tests and posts comments.

5
00:00:26.831 --> 00:00:33.440
The pipeline needs machine readable output, and the team needs feedback that is accurate enough to trust.

6
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.588
Questions come mainly from domains three and four.

7
00:00:37.988 --> 00:00:44.300
You need the headless flags for running Claude Code non interactively and getting structured output.

8
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:51.243
Then prompt design: explicit criteria for what to report, independent review instances and multi pass reviews.

9
00:00:51.243 --> 00:00:57.807
Message Batches appear as a distractor, because they suit overnight jobs, not checks that block a merge.

10
00:00:59.207 --> 00:01:00.908
Here is the typical problem.

11
00:01:00.908 --> 00:01:11.175
Developers have started ignoring the review bot because its style comments are mostly false positives, and that noise drags down trust in its accurate security findings.

12
00:01:11.175 --> 00:01:14.092
Asking it to be conservative does not help much.

13
00:01:14.092 --> 00:01:20.775
Spell out what to report and what to skip, with examples, and switch off the noisy category until it is fixed.

14
00:01:22.175 --> 00:01:23.106
Now a contrast.

15
00:01:23.106 --> 00:01:28.508
Asking the session that wrote the code to review its own work inherits its assumptions.

16
00:01:28.508 --> 00:01:31.861
A separate instance with a fresh context catches more.

17
00:01:31.861 --> 00:01:37.822
For big pull requests, review file by file and then run a separate pass for cross file problems.

18
00:01:37.822 --> 00:01:43.721
And when re-reviewing after fixes, give it the earlier findings so it reports only what is new.

19
00:01:45.121 --> 00:01:53.312
Distractors here include invented flags such as a headless environment variable, when print mode with dash p is the real mechanism.

20
00:01:53.312 --> 00:01:57.627
Scraping free text where JSON output with a schema would be reliable.

21
00:01:57.627 --> 00:02:00.378
Using batches for checks that block a merge.

22
00:02:00.378 --> 00:02:05.943
And generating tests without showing Claude the existing ones, which leads to duplicates.

23
00:02:07.343 --> 00:02:08.541
For scenario five.

24
00:02:08.541 --> 00:02:14.863
Use dash p for headless runs and JSON output with a schema when a pipeline consumes the result.

25
00:02:14.863 --> 00:02:18.523
Write explicit criteria rather than asking for caution.

26
00:02:18.523 --> 00:02:23.314
Review with an independent instance, file by file and then across files.

27
00:02:23.314 --> 00:02:28.505
And keep the Message Batches API for work that can wait, like a nightly audit.

